diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the Kath4 topology found no current fault. Phase A (L2/infrastructure): all 28 devices scanned with zero ACL drop rules, zero duplicate MAC addresses, and all 15 host machines confirmed to have both an IPv4 address and a working default route. Phase B (control plane): all nine FRR/OSPF daemons are active with healthy vtysh sockets; every router holds full OSPF adjacencies and carries exactly 17 OSPF routes — matching the 17 unique network prefixes in the topology. The routing tables of the four critical gateway devices (mswitch0, sshswitch, router16, router17) were inspected directly; each table contained all required prefixes pointing to correct next-hops with no static overrides or blackhole entries. Phase B targeted pings confirmed the three cross-group paths that the initial reachability scan had not covered: pc5 (VLAN10) → pc1 (VLAN5), pc5 (VLAN10) → pc12 (VLAN30), and pc1 (VLAN5) → pc12 (VLAN30) all returned 0% loss. Phase D (resource pressure): zero suspect devices. No routing fault, link fault, ACL block, MAC conflict, or resource contention is present in the live network state. The user's report of one "erased" group is unconfirmed by any observable evidence: there is no missing prefix, no wrong next-hop, no flap script, and no daemon or syslog trace of a past disruption. Root cause: no anomaly exists in the current network state. Fix: no corrective action is required; advise the user to re-test and verify whether their host's local ARP cache or application state has been cleared.
